    "api_error_status": null,
    "result": "Fixed! The bug was floating-point precision error in the price parsing. When JavaScript multiplies `39.95 * 100`, it gets `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995` due to how IEEE 754 floating-point numbers work.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`src/money.js`** \u2014 Instead of converting to a float and multiplying by 100, I now parse the string directly by separating euros and cents, then combining them as a string before converting to an integer.\n\n2. **`src/locale.js`** \u2014 Applied the same fix to `decimalCommaToCents` for decimal comma format prices.\n\nBoth functions now avoid floating-point arithmetic entirely, which means prices like 39.95 are parsed to exactly 3995 cents with no precision errors. All 50 tests pass, including the case that was failing.",
